==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* (''Australia, US'') [[Labor Party]]

===Etymology===
In other countries, frequently patterned or translated after the model of the {{w|British Labour Party}}, founded in 1900 to combine earlier centre-left parties.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[labour|Labour]] [[party|Party]]}}

# Any of various [[political party|political parties]] that aim to advance the interests of the [[working class]], often characterized by [[left-of-centre]] politics and support for [[organized]] [[labour]].
#: {{ux|en|Sure, the [[City]] loathed the old '''Labour Party''' that {{w|Jeremy Corbyn}} grew up with but they loved the [[Blairite]]s.}}
#* {{quote-book |en |year=1941 |author=George Orwell |authorlink=George Orwell |title={{w|The Lion and the Unicorn: Socialism and the English Genius}} |location=London |publisher=Penguin Books |year_published=1982 |isbn=978-0-14-006327-1 |page=98 |pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lionunicornsocia00orwe/page/98 |passage=The Communists had considerable influence in the '''Labour Party''' in the years 1920&ndash;26 and 1935&ndash;9. Their chief importance, and that of the whole left wing of the Labour movement, was the part they played in alienating the middle classes from Socialism.}}

